// ESLint config, added 2026-10-20. `eslint` and `eslint-config-next` had been
// devDependencies with a `lint` script since the repo was set up, but no config
// file existed — so `next lint` dropped into its interactive setup prompt and
// `next build` linted nothing. Nothing was ever checked.
//
// `next build` fails on errors and passes on warnings, so this file is now a
// gate in the same sense next.config.js's `ignoreBuildErrors: false` is. It
// starts at 0 errors.
//
// (ESLint strips comments from .eslintrc.json, so this is legal.)
{
  "extends": "next/core-web-vitals",
  "rules": {
    // OFF, and not as a backlog deferral — the rule's advice is wrong for this
    // app. All 19 sites are images on hosts we do not control: artwork from the
    // Cleveland Museum of Art, cover art from Deezer, posters from TMDB, plus
    // signed Supabase storage URLs. next/image only accepts hosts enumerated in
    // next.config.js `images.remotePatterns`, so adopting it would mean a
    // pattern per provider and a build change every time one is added.
    //
    // It is also a measured hazard rather than a hypothetical one: the Art
    // Institute of Chicago was dropped as an artwork provider precisely because
    // its image host bot-walls hotlinked requests. Routing provider images
    // through an optimizer is the kind of change that breaks them silently.
    //
    // Re-enable for any image we host ourselves and can describe.
    "@next/next/no-img-element": "off"

    // DELIBERATELY still "warn" (41 of them): react-hooks/exhaustive-deps.
    //
    // Not silenced, because the rule finds real bugs and this codebase has the
    // exact shape it catches — effects that read refs and callbacks captured
    // from a parent. But it must NOT be mass-fixed: adding a missing dep to an
    // effect that writes state derived from that dep is how you get a render
    // loop, and several of these omissions are load-bearing (BookReader's
    // registration effects use `[]` on purpose so they register once).
    //
    // Work them one at a time, each with a reason, in a session with a browser.
    //
    // Baseline re-measured 2026-11-16: 0 errors, 41 warnings, every one of them
    // this rule, over 301 linted files. The recorded figure was 47. Which six
    // went away cannot be recovered, because the old baseline was a bare total —
    // and that is the reason the split below is written out per file rather than
    // as one number. 47 → 41 is equally consistent with "six were fixed" and
    // with "twelve were fixed and six new ones appeared elsewhere", and the
    // second is the case this rule is kept on for. A total cannot tell them
    // apart; a per-file list can.
    //
    //   15  components/BookReader.tsx
    //    5  components/PageSidebar.tsx
    //    3  components/ProblemMapWidget.tsx
    //    2  app/books/[id]/page.tsx
    //    2  components/HomeFeedSection.tsx
    //    2  components/ImagePanel.tsx
    //    2  components/KnowledgeGraph.tsx
    //    2  components/StructurePanel.tsx
    //    1  app/admin/page.tsx
    //    1  app/billing/invoices/[id]/page.tsx
    //    1  app/billing/page.tsx
    //    1  components/BookmarkPanel.tsx
    //    1  components/CharacterPanel.tsx
    //    1  components/ChatPanel.tsx
    //    1  components/DiveBackIn.tsx
    //    1  components/FlashcardPanel.tsx
    //
    // BookReader holds 15 of the 41 on its own and is the one file where the
    // omissions were argued for. The 17 files under components/reader/ carry
    // none. Nothing enforces this list — it is prose, and it goes stale the
    // first time anyone writes an effect, so re-measure rather than cite it.
    //
    // To re-measure: the authoring workspaces carry only a typescript symlink,
    // so you need a real node_modules. You do NOT need a .env.local — that is
    // only `next build`, which dies collecting page data without Supabase keys.
    // Both of these run with no environment at all, and agree on 41:
    //
    //   next lint
    //   eslint --ext .ts,.tsx,.js,.jsx app components lib types middleware.ts
    //
    // `next lint` covers app/, components/ and lib/; the explicit form adds
    // types/ and middleware.ts, which are clean. Use `-f json` for the per-file
    // counts — next's own json output omits files with no messages, so it
    // cannot tell you how many files were read.
  }
}
